The Doc will contain my learnings from this project and also serve as Readme

Project : Structured_Data_Query_Engine -> Relational data storage with minimal query support

-------What this prog (db engine) does -->
Client(User of this prog[db engine]) will interact with this prog in CLI REPL
(read eval print loop)mode and first enter/create databases and tables inside them.
Then use CRUD syntax supported by our prog to ask Query on the tables he entered and get results.
The tables persist on disk in the form of  database (directory) which can have multiple tables(1 table = 2files)



------Restriction I have placed on by db engine-->
A. Data type supported by columns [int , string(255 chars max length)]
B. No parallel clients/process that will interact with dbs
C. CRUD(create read update delete) Syntax supported:-
    1. SELECT <col names> FROM <table name> INDEX_BY <expr> FILTER_BY <expr>
    2. CREATE_DB <database name>
    3. CREATE_TABLE <table_name> IN <database_name> WITH_COL<(INT,cola) ,(STR ,colb) ,(STR ,colc)>
    WITH_INDEX <colc>
    4. INSERT_ROW <cola = 55, colb="str1", colc="str2"> IN <table_name , db_name>
    5. DELETE_ROW <expr>
    6. UPDATE_ROW <cola = 55, colb="str1", colc="str2">  WHERE <expr>
INDEX_BY <expr> can be col_name >50 and col_name <100 where col_name will act as Index.
Both INDEX_BY and FILTER_BY are optional.
D. Query can Run 1 table at time ie No joins / order by (sort) 
#To support sorting use K-way External Merge sort as Cant bring full table in -memory


------ Implementation Caveats ----------->
Each Table will persit on disk in form of 2 files table.data and table.idx

---> table.data will have actual records/rows of the table stored in fixed length format
ie           Col1<4> Col2<255> Col3<255> ... 1st line header <col name and sizes>
              09       abc       bc/0
              87       d/0/0     ezv
if int then 8 bytes and if String then 255 chars use padding to keep fixed size
If wanted to support variable length then have to use the format of <len><val>
Fixed length ==> Each Row will have same size.==> Instead of scanning Each Row for end of line
in order to go to the next row (ie sequential search).We can now Randomly access each Row
with its offset in bytes[Row number * Each Row siz] (use lseek sys call to jump to that row).
table.data is append only file. Add each row to the end of the file.

----> table.idx is a B+tree on disk . Every node of a tree has some data and some pointer.
In this case Data is key-value pair. [Key = column selected as index] [Value is Row Number in table.data]
Normally Data structures are in-memory(ram) as We make nodes --> as struct objects and malloc
them. To persit them in disk we have to write the nodes in disc. Now think disc(files) are
just big char arr[][] (ie Arr of lines). We can treat them as so due to device drivers.
To write a node in disc --> soln convert struct to char[]ie serialize it.
 We can use the below format as B+tree nodes and pack it into char [] instead of using struct
| type(internal/leaf node) | nkeys | child pointers | key-value pairs |
| 2B                       |2B     | nkeys * 8B     | nkeys * 2*8B |

Now each node of the B-tree will be a each line in the file table.idx

Terminology --> File --Array of Blocks/Nodes/Pages
B-tree has Blocks/Nodes/Pages . 1 Node size = INT * 1 OS page size(4096B).

Disk IO reads occurs 512B at a time (1 sector at a time).
OS buffers them(disk IO) and keeps them in a cache known as OS page.

Why B+tree (Height balanced n-ary tree) --> Each level of the tree is a disk read in
a lookup, and n-ary trees are shorter than binary trees for the same number
of keys(n) (lognN vs. log2N), thus n-ary trees are used for fewer disk reads per
lookup , plus Height balanced --> Always lognN search.

Use Fsync when writing to file :-        fsync()  transfers  ("flushes")  all  modified in-core data of (i.e., modified buffer cache pages for) the file re‐
       ferred to by the file descriptor fd to the disk device (or other permanent storage device) so that all changed  in‐
       formation  can be retrieved even if the system crashes or is rebooted.  This includes writing through or flushing a
       disk cache if present.  The call blocks until the device reports that the transfer has completed.
Avoid in-place updates by renaming files and then fsync on parent dir

Search Query Processing -->
Step1 : Parse the Search query and find the apt key
Step2 : Search the apt key in (B+tree) and get the Row id in table.data as value
Step3 : Fetch the row using row-id and display it as result


------------- About B+tree Implementation -----------------------
Here's my proposed approach to create a durable, consistent, disk-based B-Tree.

This approach is based on using a single file, fixed-size blocks (pages), shadow paging (copy-on-write) for consistency, and a metadata header to manage the tree.

1. File and Metadata Format
We'll use a single file (e.g., table.idx). The file will be divided into fixed-size blocks, for example, 4096 bytes (4KB). This is the standard page size for most operating systems and databases, and it simplifies I/O.

Block 0: The Metadata Header--> This block is special. It's always at offset 0 and stores the global information about the tree. It's the only block we'll ever (carefully) overwrite in place.

Blocks 1...N: Node Data --> All other blocks in the file will store the B-Tree nodes (one node per block).

Metadata Header (Block 0) Layout
This block will contain:

Magic Number (e.g., 8 bytes): A custom byte sequence like "\xDE\xAD\xBE\xEFBTR3" to identify this file as our B-Tree.

Key Type (1 byte): A flag to define the key type for this entire tree.

0x00: Keys are int (4 bytes).

0x01: Keys are char[255] (255 bytes).

(Note: A single tree cannot mix key types. You create either an int-keyed tree or a string-keyed tree.)

Minimum Degree t (4 bytes): The minimum degree of the B-Tree. This value will be calculated when the tree is created, based on the key_type and block_size.

Root Node Offset (8 bytes): The long long file offset (e.g., 4096, 8192, etc.) of the current root node. This is the most critical piece of data.

Free List Head Offset (8 bytes): The file offset of the first block in a linked-list of free blocks (for block reuse). 0 means no free blocks.

2. Node Format (Serialization)
Each node will be read/written as a single 4096-byte block. The C++ BTreeNode class will be replaced by a NodePage struct/class that is purely for serialization. It will not contain new/delete or pointers, but rather fixed-size arrays.

Pointers (BTreeNode** children) will be replaced with 8-byte file offsets.

Node Block Layout (4096 bytes)
Node Header (e.g., 16 bytes)

is_leaf (1 byte): 1 if leaf, 0 if internal.

n (4 bytes): The current number of keys in this node.

(Padding, 11 bytes): To align the following data.

Data Arrays This is the main body of the node. The layout depends on the key_type stored in the metadata header. The t value dictates the size of these arrays.

Array 1: Child Offsets (Size: 2t * 8 bytes)

An array of 2t long long file offsets.

This is the first array, as it's present in all nodes (leaf or internal, though leaves just won't use it).

Array 2: Values (Size: (2t - 1) * 4 bytes)

An array of (2t - 1) int values.

Array 3: Keys (Size: depends on key_type)

If key_type == 0 (int): An array of (2t - 1) int keys. (Size: (2t - 1) * 4 bytes)

If key_type == 1 (string): An array of (2t - 1) char[255] keys. (Size: (2t - 1) * 255 bytes)

Free/Unused Space: The rest of the 4096-byte block.

When a tree is created (e.g., BTree::create("db_file", BTree::KEY_STRING)), we will calculate the largest t that allows all these arrays to fit within 4096 - 16 bytes and store that t in the metadata header.

3. Durability & Consistency Approach
This is the most critical part. If we just overwrite nodes in place, a power failure during an operation (like a node split) will leave the file in a corrupted, inconsistent state.

My proposed approach is Shadow Paging (Copy-on-Write).

This method never overwrites a "live" node.

How it Works (e.g., for an Insert):
Find Path: We read the root_node_offset from the metadata header (Block 0). We traverse the tree by reading nodes from their file offsets (e.g., root at 4096, child at 8192, leaf at 12288). We keep this path in memory.

Modify (Copy-on-Write): We find the leaf node (at 12288) where the key should be inserted.

We do not modify the node at 12288.

Instead, we copy its data into a new in-memory buffer, add the new key/value, and write this modified node to a brand-new, unused block at the end of the file (or from the free list), e.g., at offset 16384.

Propagate Up:

The parent node (at 8192) is now out-of-date. Its child pointer still says 12288, but it needs to point to our new node at 16384.

So, we copy-on-write the parent. We read 8192, make a copy in memory, update the child offset from 12288 to 16384, and write this modified parent to another new block, e.g., at offset 20480.

Propagate to Root:

This process repeats all the way up to the root. The original root (at 4096) is copied, its child pointer is updated (from 8192 to 20480), and this new root node is written to a new block, e.g., at offset 24576.

Atomic Commit (The "Flip"):

At this point, the entire old tree (starting at offset 4096) is still untouched and perfectly valid on disk. We have just built a complete new tree branch in new blocks.

The only thing left to do is make the new tree "live".

We perform a single, atomic write to the Metadata Header (Block 0). We update the root_node_offset from 4096 to 24576.

We then immediately call fsync() on the file handle to force this one-block change to the physical disk.

Crash-Safety Analysis:
If power fails before the "Flip" (Step 5):

No problem. When we restart, we read Block 0. The root_node_offset is still 4096. The old, consistent tree is all we see. The new blocks (16384, 20480, 24576) are just garbage data at the end of the file that will eventually be reclaimed. The insert operation is simply lost (which is the correct "rollback" behavior).

If power fails after the "Flip" (Step 5):

No problem. When we restart, we read Block 0. The root_node_offset is 24576. This points to our new, valid root, which points to the new, valid branch. The insert is successful. The old blocks (4096, 8192, 12288) are now garbage and can be added to the free list.

This copy-on-write mechanism provides perfect ACI (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation) for each operation. Durability is provided by the fsync() call on the metadata block.


PagedFile: A class to manage the file, block allocation, and the free list.

Node: A class that can serialize/deserialize to/from a 4KB byte buffer.

BTree: The main class, modified to use  PagedFile and to implement the shadow paging logic instead of in-memory pointers.


--------------------- How Free List Works -------------------------------
Need of Free List --> On disk data structure to manage stale blocks leaked by COW

The Two Key Components
The "Head" Pointer (in Metadata): The only thing we store in our main MetadataHeader (Block 0) is a single offset_t (8 bytes) called free_list_head.

If free_list_head == 0, the list is empty.

If free_list_head != 0, it holds the file offset (e.g., 0x3000) of the first block in the free list.

The "Next" Pointer (inside the free block): When a block is not being used to store B-Tree node data, we re-purpose its first 8 bytes. We use these bytes to store the offset_t of the next free block in the chain.

The rest of the 4088 bytes in that free block are just garbage; we don't care about them.

How the List is Managed
Here is the step-by-step logic for the two operations that manage the list.

1. Operation: freeBlock(0x2000) (Adding a block to the list)
Let's say our Copy-on-Write logic just finished and 0x2000 is now a "stale" block that needs to be recycled.

Current State: metadata.free_list_head points to 0x5000.

Metadata (Block 0):
[ free_list_head: 0x5000 ]
Step 1: The freeBlock(0x2000) function is called.

Step 2: It reads the current head: old_head = 0x5000.

Step 3: It goes to our newly freed block (0x2000) and writes the old_head value (0x5000) into its first 8 bytes. This "links" our new block to the start of the old list.

File Block at 0x2000:
[ 0x0000000000005000 | ...garbage... ]
(This block now "points" to block 0x5000)
Step 4: It updates the free_list_head (in memory) to point to our new block.

Metadata (In-Memory):
[ free_list_head: 0x2000 ]
Result: We've successfully "pushed" the old block onto the front of the free list. The list now looks like: 0x2000 -> 0x5000 -> ...

2. Operation: allocateBlock() (Removing a block from the list)
Now, a new insert operation needs a fresh block to write to. It calls allocateBlock().

Current State: metadata.free_list_head points to 0x2000.

Metadata (In-Memory):
[ free_list_head: 0x2000 ]

File Block at 0x2000:
[ 0x0000000000005000 | ...garbage... ]
Step 1: The function checks metadata.free_list_head. It's 0x2000 (not empty).

Step 2: This is the block we will reuse. We store this value: block_to_return = 0x2000.

Step 3: This is the key: How do we find the next block? We read the first 8 bytes of the block we are about to return.

It reads 0x2000 and finds the value 0x5000 stored there.

Step 4: It updates the free_list_head (in memory) to this "next" value.

Metadata (In-Memory):
[ free_list_head: 0x5000 ]
Step 5: It returns block_to_return (0x2000) to the caller.

Result: The caller (writeNode) gets the offset 0x2000. It then immediately overwrites the entire 4KB block with its B-Tree node data. The old "next" pointer (0x5000) that was stored there is completely erased, which is fine because that block is now "live" and no longer part of the free list.

This whole process is invisible to the B-Tree logic, which just sees allocateBlock() as a "black box" that gives it a valid, empty block to write to.


----------------------- Free List with COW ---------------------------
Because of CoW, every successful insert or update, no matter how small, must propagate all the way to the root.

Here is the chain reaction:

You want to insert (5, 50) into a leaf node (Leaf_A at offset 0x1000).

We read Leaf_A into memory. We see it's not full.

We add (5, 50) to the in-memory copy.

Now, we must write this modified node to a new block. We call writeNode(), which allocates a new block (e.g., 0x5000) and writes the modified leaf data there.

We return 0x5000 to the parent node's recursive call.

The parent node (Parent_A at offset 0x2000) now has a problem. Its child pointer still says 0x1000, but the child's data is now at 0x5000.

The parent must be updated. We read Parent_A, change the child pointer from 0x1000 to 0x5000, and write this modified parent to a new block (0x6000). We also free the old leaf block (0x1000).

This change now propagates to its parent, and so on, all the way to the root.

The root_offset in the metadata header is finally updated to point to the new root, and we free the old parent block (0x2000).

Conclusion: In this specific CoW model, there is no such thing as a "partial write." A change to any block (even just one byte) creates a new block, which changes its offset, which requires its parent to be updated. This ripples all the way to the root.


-------------- Bug with the above Free List Implement :-
During Insert:- As soon as Parent update its child pointer . Add child to freeList
Now when writing grandparent --> file.allocateBlock() consults freeList and gives the child block
ie grandparent gets written in child block ---> ie Cant recover if crash

As the 1st 8bytes of the stale block(free list block) is used to contain the next pointer
of the free list , in case of recover --> data corruct as stale blocks(old datas) 1st 8 byte corrupt
Hint we can only use 8bytes of old data when we have succesfully upated root to new root

Soln ---->2 phase commit
trade-off is that a crash might leak blocks, but it will never corrupt the database.
To solve this, we will introduce a new in-memory-only list.

DbFile Class Change: We will add a std::vector<offset_t> pending_free_list; to the DbFile class. This list is not part of the on-disk header. It's a temporary, in-memory "to-do" list for garbage collection.

2. How the Transaction Works (The CoW)
This is how an insert or remove will now function:

freeBlock(offset_t offset) is changed:

It is now a trivial, in-memory-only operation.

It does NOT write to the disk.

It does NOT touch the header.free_list_head.

It ONLY does this->pending_free_list.push_back(offset);.

This "stages" the old block for future garbage collection, without affecting the on-disk tree.

allocateBlock() is now safe:

It ONLY gets blocks from the committed header.free_list_head.

Since freeBlock no longer adds to this list during a transaction, allocateBlock can never see or re-allocate a block that was just freed.

This solves the "reuse-after-free" corruption bug completely.

A new commit function commitRootAndGarbageCollect(offset_t new_root_offset) is created: This function will now be the only way to commit, and it works in two distinct phases.

3. The New Commit: Phase 1 (Data Commit)
This phase's only job is to make the new data "live."

commitRootAndGarbageCollect is called with the new_root_offset.

It updates the in-memory metadata: this->header.root_offset = new_root_offset;.

It does NOT touch the header.free_list_head yet.

It writes the entire header (with the new root and the old free list head) to Block 0 and calls file.flush().

Crash Analysis (Phase 1):

If we crash before this flush: On restart, we read Block 0. We get the old root_offset and the old free_list_head. The database is in its original, perfect state. The new blocks are garbage on disk, and the pending_free_list (which was in memory) is gone. No corruption.

If we crash after this flush: On restart, we read Block 0. We get the new root_offset and the old free_list_head. The new tree is live and 100% valid. The blocks in the (now-lost) pending_free_list are now "leaked" (orphaned). This is a block leak, but it is NOT corruption. The database is perfectly safe and correct.

4. The New Commit: Phase 2 (Garbage Collection)
Only after Phase 1 is successful, the function proceeds to Phase 2.

The pending_free_list (still in memory) contains all the blocks that are now safely leaked.

We will now add them to the real free list.

The function iterates through every offset in pending_free_list:

It reads the current header.free_list_head value.

It safely writes this value ("next" pointer) into the first 8 bytes of the block at offset.

It updates the in-memory header.free_list_head = offset;.

After this loop finishes, the header.free_list_head in memory now points to the start of a complete, valid chain of newly-freed blocks.

The function performs a second commit: It calls writeHeader() and file.flush() again, this time committing the new free_list_head to disk.

Finally, it clears the in-memory pending_free_list.

Crash Analysis (Phase 2):

If we crash during this process: On restart, we read Block 0. We get the new root_offset (from Phase 1, which succeeded) and the old free_list_head (because the second commit never finished). The blocks we were trying to free are simply... still leaked. Again, no corruption. The database is 100% safe.

---------------------------------------------------------------
***********************How B-tree Insert Works 
Scenario 1: Simple Insert (Leaf has space)
This is the most common case, equivalent to a simple leaf deletion. The key insight here, which is fundamental to our CoW design, is that even a "simple" insert that doesn't cause a split still causes a full rewrite of the entire path from the leaf to the root.

Let's trace the insertion of a new key (4, 40).

Initial State: The tree is valid. The path to the correct leaf is Root_A (0x1000) -> Parent_A (0x2000) -> Leaf_A (0x3000). Leaf_A is not full.

Transaction Goal: Insert (4, 40) into Leaf_A.

1. Find Path:

insertRecursive calls insertNonFull, which traverses the tree. It arrives at Parent_A (0x2000) and determines it must descend into Leaf_A (0x3000).

It checks if Leaf_A is full. It is not.

2. Copy-on-Write (The Leaf):

The function reads Leaf_A (0x3000) into memory.

It adds the new key (4, 40) to the in-memory copy.

It calls writeNode() for this modified leaf.

writeNode() calls allocateBlock(), which returns a new block, e.g., 0x8000.

The modified leaf (now Leaf_B) is written to 0x8000.

3. Free List (Staging):

insertNonFull (at the leaf level) returns the new offset 0x8000 to its caller.

The caller (the insertNonFull for Parent_A) sees that old_child_offset (0x3000) != new_child_offset (0x8000).

This change must be reflected in the parent. The old leaf block is now stale.

The parent's insertNonFull function calls file.freeBlock(0x3000).

This adds 0x3000 to the in-memory pending_free_list.

4. Propagate Up (CoW):

The insertNonFull for Parent_A (at 0x2000) now has an invalid child pointer.

It updates its in-memory copy, changing the child pointer from 0x3000 to 0x8000.

It calls writeNode() for this modified parent.

writeNode() gets a new block (e.g., 0x9000) and writes the modified parent (Parent_B) to it.

The function then calls file.freeBlock(0x2000), adding the old parent to the pending_free_list.

5. Commit (The Two Phases):

This CoW rewrite continues all the way up to the root, creating a new root (Root_B at 0xA000) and freeing the old root (Root_A at 0x1000).

The top-level insert function receives the final new root offset, 0xA000.

It calls file.commitRootAndGarbageCollect(0xA000).

Phase 1 (Data Commit): The file header (Block 0) is updated: root_offset = 0xA000. This is flushed to disk.

At this instant, the new tree is "live." The old blocks (0x1000, 0x2000, 0x3000) are all orphaned (leaked), but not corrupt.

Phase 2 (Garbage Commit): The commitFreeList function runs. It takes [0x1000, 0x2000, 0x3000] from the pending_free_list, writes the "next" pointers into them, and commits the new free_list_head = 0x1000 to Block 0. The old blocks are now safely recycled.

Scenario 2: Complex Insert (Leaf is full, causing a split)
This is the counterpart to the deletion merge.

Initial State: Path is Root_A (0x1000) -> Parent_A (0x2000). The insert function must descend into Child_A (0x3000).

Transaction Goal: Insert key (25, 250).

Trigger: The insertNonFull for Parent_A checks its child (Child_A at 0x3000) and finds it is full (node->n == 2*t - 1).

1. Call splitChild():

Before it can descend, insertNonFull must call splitChild.

splitChild is called with the parent (Parent_A at 0x2000) and the index of the full child (Child_A at 0x3000).

2. Copy-on-Write (The Split):

splitChild reads Parent_A (0x2000) and Child_A (0x3000) into memory.

It creates three new nodes in memory:

Parent_B: The parent, now with the middle key from Child_A added.

Left_Child_B: The left half of the original Child_A.

Right_Sibling_Z: A new node with the right half of Child_A.

It calls writeNode() for all three new nodes:

Right_Sibling_Z is written to a new block (e.g., 0x8000).

Left_Child_B is written to a new block (e.g., 0x9000).

Parent_B (with updated child pointers to 0x9000 and 0x8000) is written to a new block (e.g., 0xA000).

3. Free List (Staging):

splitChild has successfully created three new blocks, making the two old ones stale.

It calls file.freeBlock(0x2000) (Old Parent_A).

It calls file.freeBlock(0x3000) (Old Child_A).

Both offsets are added to the in-memory pending_free_list.

splitChild returns the new parent's offset (0xA000) to insertNonFull.

4. Continue Insertion (now just Scenario 1):

insertNonFull (for the original parent) now has the new parent offset 0xA000.

It reads Parent_B (at 0xA000) and decides which of its new children (at 0x9000 or 0x8000) to descend into. Let's say it's Right_Sibling_Z (at 0x8000).

It calls insertNonFull on Right_Sibling_Z. This is now just a Simple Insert (Scenario 1).

This recursive call will:

Modify Right_Sibling_Z (at 0x8000).

Write it to a new block 0xB000.

Call file.freeBlock(0x8000).

This change propagates up, causing Parent_B (at 0xA000) to be rewritten to Parent_C (at 0xC000) and file.freeBlock(0xA000).

5. Commit (The Two Phases):

This chain of CoW rewrites and freeBlock staging operations continues all the way to the root, finally returning a final_root_offset (e.g., 0xF000) to the top-level insert function.

The pending_free_list now contains a long list of all the old, stale blocks: [0x2000, 0x3000, 0x8000, 0xA000, ..., 0x1000].

The insert function calls file.commitRootAndGarbageCollect(0xF000).

Phase 1 (Data): Commits root_offset = 0xF000. The new, split-and-inserted tree is now live.

Phase 2 (Garbage): commitFreeList runs, finds the entire list of staged offsets, and safely recycles all of them.

--------------------------- How Btree Delete works ----------------
Scenario 1: Simple Leaf Deletion (e.g., deleting key 6 from a leaf)
Read Path: The removeRecursive function traverses the tree (e.g., Root_A -> Parent_A -> Leaf_A) to find the key. Leaf_A is at offset 0x3000.

Copy-on-Write (CoW):

We read the block for Leaf_A (at 0x3000) into memory.

We remove the key (6) from the in-memory copy.

We call writeNode(), which gets a new block (0x8000) from allocateBlock().

We write our modified leaf (now Leaf_B) to this new block, 0x8000.

Free List (Staging):

removeFromLeaf returns the new offset (0x8000) to its caller (removeRecursive).

The caller sees that the child's offset has changed (from 0x3000 to 0x8000).

It now "frees" the old, stale block by calling file.freeBlock(0x3000).

This only adds 0x3000 to the in-memory pending_free_list.

Propagate Up (CoW):

This change must propagate up. The parent (Parent_A at 0x2000) is now invalid because its child pointer still says 0x3000.

We read Parent_A, update its child pointer to 0x8000, and write this modified parent to a new block, Parent_B (at 0x9000).

The code then calls file.freeBlock(0x2000), adding the old parent to the pending_free_list.

Commit (The Two Phases):

This continues all the way to the root. The top-level remove function gets the final new root offset (e.g., 0xA000).

It calls file.commitRootAndGarbageCollect(0xA000).

Phase 1 (Data): The new root_offset = 0xA000 is written to Block 0 and flushed. The new tree is now live. The old blocks (0x3000, 0x2000, etc.) are now officially "orphaned."

Phase 2 (Garbage): The commitFreeList function runs. It takes the pending_free_list and safely adds all those orphaned blocks to the on-disk free list, committing the new free_list_head to Block 0.

***************Scenario 2: Complex Deletion (Causing a Merge)
This is the exact same process, just on a larger scale.

Trigger: removeRecursive descends to a Parent_A (at 0x2000) and sees that the child it needs to visit (Child_L at 0x3000) is "under-full" (has t-1 keys).

fill() is called: It decides to merge() Child_L with its sibling Child_R (at 0x4000).

Copy-on-Write (The Merge):

The merge function reads three blocks into memory: Parent_A (at 0x2000), Child_L (at 0x3000), and Child_R (at 0x4000).

It creates two new nodes in memory:

Merged_Child: A big node containing all keys from L, R, and the key from the parent.

Parent_B: The parent node, but with one less key and one less child pointer.

Write New Blocks:

writeNode() is called for Merged_Child, writing it to a new block (e.g., 0xB000).

writeNode() is called for Parent_B, writing it to a new block (e.g., 0xC000).

Free List (Staging):

The merge function now has the new parent offset (0xC000) and can safely free all the old blocks it used.

file.freeBlock(0x2000); (Old Parent)

file.freeBlock(0x3000); (Old Child_L)

file.freeBlock(0x4000); (Old Child_R)

All three offsets are now staged in the pending_free_list.

Propagate and Commit:

The merge function returns the new parent offset (0xC000) to removeRecursive.

This change propagates up to the root, and the final commit is handled by the top-level remove function, just like in Scenario 1. The two-phase commit safely garbage-collects all three old blocks.
------------------------------------------------------------------------
